import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.net.URL;

public class SecureWebRequest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            URL url = new URL("http://example.com");
            // Using HttpURLConnection to avoid direct stream access
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("GET");
            BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(connection.getInputStream()));
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
            reader.close();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
***
In the first code snippet, the direct access to the stream from the URL object can lead to a vulnerability known as a "man-in-the-middle" attack, where an attacker could potentially intercept the data being transferred. This is because the stream is not secured and can be intercepted by an attacker if the connection is not encrypted. In the second snippet, we use `HttpURLConnection` which is a safer way to handle HTTP requests as it provides a layer of abstraction and security over the raw stream. Additionally, it allows for setting request methods and handling responses properly.

